From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:36:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10920/11171] Record a 13-block cascade whose root I cleared as
 benign an hour earlier

At 17:35Z I validated exp6942, reported it clean and unflagged, and said the
conductor logging a bare blocked_ verdict as OK was correct behaviour. True
about the artifact's honesty, and it missed the consequence completely. The same
JSON carried v608_execution_contract_ready_score = 0 and ten downstream tasks
gate on that field. I read the artifact in isolation.

Between 17:37 and 18:26 that produced thirteen gate-blocks and killed ten of
milestone 608's twelve tasks: exp6943 and exp6948 each blocked three times
against exp6942's gate field and retired, and exp6944 through exp6951 skipped
pre-emptively on retired upstreams, with the capstone failing last.

Base rate measured before characterising it. GATE_BLOCK by day runs 41, 8, 16,
2, 8 and today's 19, so the volume is unremarkable. What the daily count cannot
show, and what matters, is that thirteen of today's blocks share one root.

Also notes the capstone's FAIL is the same misleading
artifact_not_updated_past_bootstrap message recorded earlier today, this time
with a legitimate trigger: a partial_ verdict genuinely lacks a terminal prefix,
even though the artifact was written and is clean.

The useful output is a check, not a lesson. An artifact with a ready_score of 0
is unremarkable; the same artifact when the ACTIVE roadmap gates on that field is
a pending cascade, and the two are separable by joining two files the dashboard
already reads, with no model involved. Proposed with its inputs named rather than
built, and paired with the interim rule: summarize_artifact tells you an artifact
is honest, not whether anything depends on it.

